From bcf2adef66458bad4ae215723189f014176edb16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BOAR R&D <noreply>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:43:02 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] routing: send code requests in a chat to the code path,
 and search a follow-up with the conversation's subject

In a multi-turn chat the rules lost both the intent and the topic. "What can a smart contract do?" -> "what it look like?" ->
"i want to see the code" -> "can you write it?" ended with the model refusing to write code: the code rule only knew
"write (a|some) code", so "write it", "show me the code" and "not even an example?" went to lookup/chat with retrieval
and the 2-3 sentence answer, and the follow-up search took only the immediately previous turn, which from the third turn
on has no subject of its own ("what it look like?").

Two changes, no prompt change:
- classify.ts: classifyTaskInContext. An explicit code noun ("show me the code", "in Python") is a code request; the
  ambiguous forms ("write it", "an example", "what does it look like") are code only when this message or an earlier user
  turn has a technical word (smart contract, algorithm, function, Python, ...), so "an example of a supervolcano" stays a
  lookup. answer.ts uses it for the rules' task type and skips the route gate when it decides code (a SEARCH verdict would
  turn the request back into a chat with retrieval).
- followUp.ts (the follow-up search planner from fullnative-dev, the TypeScript twin of FollowUp.kt): a follow-up is
  anchored on the latest earlier question that names its own subject, else on the first question, instead of on the turn
  just before it. answer.ts now searches through it.

Neither applies in a health or emergency conversation (a detector fire, health or safety words or a card condition in this
or any earlier user turn, a health follow-up): first-aid chats keep exactly the route and the search they had.

Measured on a 30-chat / 159-turn multi-turn probe set (49 capability asks), deterministic replay: intent accuracy
74.8% -> 85.5%, code asks routed to code 0/21 -> 17/21, capability asks left on a retrieval + 2-3 sentence route
45/49 -> 28/49; 0 class changes on control chats and on first-aid chats; 0 changes on 144 single-turn guard questions
(60 public bench questions, 30 emergency and 54 pack questions). With Qwen3-4B generating, this routing plus a prompt
line raised fulfilled capability turns 0.633 -> 0.755 (a hand audit puts the real gain near +0.10); the prompt line
alone gave a third of it and is not part of this change, because it added wrong first-aid steps.

Tests: code asks in a chat, the follow-up anchor, and first-aid follow-ups that keep their route and search.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply>
